This is actually a very good interview question because it tests whether you understand JavaScript fundamentals vs Framework Design Patterns.

There are two common ways you'll see Playwright frameworks built:

Approach 1: Class-Based Framework (Recommended)

Example:

class LoginPage {

    constructor(page) {
        this.page = page;
        this.userName = page.locator('#user');
        this.password = page.locator('#pass');
        this.loginBtn = page.locator('#login');
    }

    async login(user, pass) {
        await this.userName.fill(user);
        await this.password.fill(pass);
        await this.loginBtn.click();
    }
}

module.exports = { LoginPage };

Usage:

const { LoginPage } = require('./LoginPage');

const loginPage = new LoginPage(page);

await loginPage.login("admin","admin123");
Advantages

✅ Encapsulation

this.userName
this.password
this.loginBtn

belong to LoginPage only.

✅ Reusability

One object can be reused throughout the test.

const loginPage = new LoginPage(page);

✅ Supports Inheritance

class BasePage {
   async goto(url){}
}

class LoginPage extends BasePage {
}

✅ Better for Large Frameworks

