Approach 2: Pure JavaScript Module Exports

Example:

async function login(page,user,pass){

   await page.locator('#user').fill(user);
   await page.locator('#pass').fill(pass);
   await page.locator('#login').click();
}

module.exports = {
   login
};

Usage:

const loginActions = require('./loginActions');

await loginActions.login(
    page,
    "admin",
    "admin123"
);
Advantages

✅ Simpler

✅ Less code

✅ Easier for small projects

Disadvantages

You repeatedly pass page:

login(page,user,pass)
logout(page)
search(page,product)

Every method needs page.

Constructor Benefit

Without Constructor:

async login(page,user,pass)

Need page every time.

With Constructor:

constructor(page){
   this.page = page;
}

Now:

async login(user,pass)

Cleaner.

Deloitte Interview Answer

If asked:

Why did you use classes and constructors in your Playwright framework?

Answer:

I used a class-based Page Object Model because it provides encapsulation, reusability, and maintainability. The constructor initializes page and locators once when the object is created, eliminating the need to pass the page object repeatedly. This approach also supports inheritance through a BasePage and scales well for large automation frameworks.

